================================================================================
WHY NORMALIZE SUBMITTED VALUES?
================================================================================

Suppose the user enters:

firstName:

"  John  "


email:

"John@Example.COM"


The controlled form can preserve exactly what the user types while editing.

At submission, normalized application data may become:

firstName:

"John"


email:

"john@example.com"


This separates:

EDITING STATE

from:

STORED APPLICATION RECORD


================================================================================
WHY AGE REMAINS A STRING DURING EDITING
================================================================================

For:

<input type="number">


event.target.value generally provides a string.

Examples:

"18"

"42"

""


Keeping age as a string while editing supports the empty-input state:

age:
''


After successful validation:

age:
Number(formData.age)


So the person record stores a number.


================================================================================
WHY USE A FUNCTIONAL PEOPLE UPDATE?
================================================================================

The next people list depends on the existing list.

Use:

setPeople(previousPeople => [

  newPerson,

  ...previousPeople

])


This avoids depending on a possibly stale `people` value from the render that
created the handler.


================================================================================
WHY USE crypto.randomUUID()?
================================================================================

Each card needs stable identity.

Person object:

{
  id:
    crypto.randomUUID(),

  ...
}


Rendering:

<PersonCard
  key={person.id}
/>


The React key belongs where map() creates each list element/component.


