WHY RETURN AN ERRORS OBJECT?
================================================================================

The errors object supports:

• Looking up an error by field name

• Applying invalid styling

• Connecting messages with fields

• Deciding whether submission can continue


Example:

errors.email


matches:

formData.email


and:

name="email"


================================================================================
VALIDATION CHECK
================================================================================

const errors =
  validatePersonForm(
    formData
  )


const isValid =
  Object.keys(
    errors
  ).length === 0


If:

isValid === true

process the data.


If:

isValid === false

display the errors.


================================================================================
TRIM BEFORE LENGTH CHECK
================================================================================

Less reliable:

formData.firstName.length < 2


User input:

" A "


Raw length:

3


Trimmed value:

"A"


Trimmed length:

1


Therefore validate:

formData.firstName
  .trim()
  .length


This checks meaningful content instead of surrounding whitespace.


================================================================================
PHONE VALIDATION
================================================================================

A basic teaching approach:

const phoneDigits =
  formData.phone
    .replace(/\D/g, '')


Then check digit count:

phoneDigits.length


This allows formatting characters such as:

Spaces

Hyphens

Parentheses

Plus signs


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Phone-number validation is country-specific.

A production application should use rules appropriate to the supported
countries and product requirements.


================================================================================
EMAIL VALIDATION
================================================================================

The lesson uses a basic structure check:

/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/


This is suitable as a simple client-side teaching rule.

It is not proof that:

• The address exists

• The mailbox can receive email

• The user owns the address


Verification may require a backend confirmation workflow.


================================================================================
AGE VALIDATION
================================================================================

Form value:

formData.age

is a string during editing.


Conversion:

const numericAge =
  Number(formData.age)


Checks:

• Empty?

• Whole number?

• At least 18?

• Not above 120?


IMPORTANT:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The age limits are example product rules.

They are not universal rules for every form.


================================================================================
CLIENT AND SERVER VALIDATION
================================================================================

CLIENT VALIDATION:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Improves immediate user feedback

• Prevents obviously invalid local processing

• Helps users correct mistakes


SERVER VALIDATION:
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

• Enforces trusted business rules

• Handles authoritative data checks

• Protects backend operations

• Must not trust client input automatically


MEMORY:

Client Validation
      +
Server Validation
      =
Reliable Workflow


